= A6
{c}
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=A6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Treat each species as a dilute classical ideal gas: molecules of the same species are indistinguishable, while different species are distinguishable. Let $N_i$ be the number of molecules of species $i$, $q_i(T,V)$ its <molecular partition function> with energies measured from a chosen internal reference, and $\varepsilon^{0,i}$ that reference energy per molecule relative to a common chemical energy convention. Then the mixture's <canonical partition function> factorizes:
$$
Q=\prod_i\frac{q_i^{N_i}}{N_i!}\exp[-\beta N_i\varepsilon^{0,i}],
\qquad \beta=\frac1{k_{\mathrm B}T}.
$$
The $N_i!$ corrects the overcounting of <indistinguishable particles>. The exponential restores the species-dependent reference energies that matter for reactions. Thus the <Helmholtz free energy> is
$$
A=-k_{\mathrm B}T\sum_i\left[N_i\ln q_i-\ln(N_i!)\right]
+\sum_iN_i\varepsilon^{0,i}.
$$
Using <Stirling's approximation>, $\ln(N_i!)\simeq N_i\ln N_i-N_i$, and differentiating at fixed $T,V,N_{j\ne i}$ yields
$$
\boxed{\mu_i=\left(\frac{\partial A}{\partial N_i}\right)_{T,V,N_{j\ne i}}
=-k_{\mathrm B}T\ln\frac{q_i}{N_i}+\varepsilon^{0,i}.}
$$
The ideal-gas $q_i$ does not depend on the number of molecules, so no additional $\partial q_i/\partial N_i$ term appears. These are chemical potentials per molecule; multiply by the <Avogadro constant> for a molar chemical potential. The result assumes the thermodynamic, nondegenerate-gas limit.

= b
{parent=A6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $\rho_i=N_i/V$ be a molecular <number density> and define the volume-independent <molecular partition function density> $f_i=q_i/V$. Then $q_i/N_i=f_i/\rho_i$. Also define the reaction reference-energy difference
$$
\Delta\varepsilon_0=\varepsilon^{0,\mathrm C}-\varepsilon^{0,\mathrm A}-\varepsilon^{0,\mathrm B}.
$$
Substitution into the equilibrium chemical-potential relation gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=-k_{\mathrm B}T\ln\frac{f_{\mathrm C}}{\rho_{\mathrm C}}
+k_{\mathrm B}T\ln\frac{f_{\mathrm A}}{\rho_{\mathrm A}}
+k_{\mathrm B}T\ln\frac{f_{\mathrm B}}{\rho_{\mathrm B}}
+\Delta\varepsilon_0,\\
\frac{\rho_{\mathrm C}}{\rho_{\mathrm A}\rho_{\mathrm B}}
&=\frac{f_{\mathrm C}}{f_{\mathrm A}f_{\mathrm B}}
\exp\left(-\frac{\Delta\varepsilon_0}{k_{\mathrm B}T}\right).
\end{aligned}
$$
An <equilibrium constant> expressed using concentration activities $a_i=\rho_i/c^\circ$ is dimensionless:
$$
\boxed{K_c=\frac{a_{\mathrm C}}{a_{\mathrm A}a_{\mathrm B}}
=\frac{\rho_{\mathrm C}c^\circ}{\rho_{\mathrm A}\rho_{\mathrm B}}
=\frac{f_{\mathrm C}c^\circ}{f_{\mathrm A}f_{\mathrm B}}e^{-\Delta\varepsilon_0/(k_{\mathrm B}T)}.}
$$
Here every $f_i$ has units $\mathrm{m^{-3}}$, and $c^\circ$ is the chosen <standard concentration>, initially one molecule per cubic metre. The factor $c^\circ$ is essential: without it the association quotient has units of volume.

